<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Hungary</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> is the historical name for the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and parts of north-eastern Hungary, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Kassa</a> (Košice) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pozsony</a> (Bratislava). Its mining towns produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold. In the 17th century it was a centre of Protestant and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuruc">Kuruc</a> resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and in 1682 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> as its ruler.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-hungary.jpg" alt="Upper Hungary" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Braun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kosice_by_Braun_Hogenberg_(middle_size)_(97692526).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region had several names. Hungarian used Felső-Magyarország, literally Upper Hungary, and Felvidék, the Upland, which could also mean the mountainous north as against the southern lowlands; Slovak used Horná zem and Horné Uhorsko, and German Oberungarn. Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> spoke of the Partes regni superiores, the upper parts of the kingdom, from which the later name arose, so its use for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> is somewhat anachronistic. The Slovaks called the land they lived in Slovensko, a word found in documents from the 15th century, though it had no legal or political status of its own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The term came into use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in the 16th century. It then meant present-day eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and neighbouring parts of Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> that the Ottomans did not hold, which formed the Captaincy of Upper Hungary, a military district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Kassa</a> from 1564 to 1686. Western Slovakia was then called Lower Hungary, and the mining school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banska-stiavnica">Selmecbánya</a>, now Banská Štiavnica, was still described as lying there in the 18th century. In the 1680s the region was briefly an Ottoman vassal state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a>.</p><p>From around 1800 until 1920 the name covered the whole north of the kingdom beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>: present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a> and the counties of Borsod-Abaúj-Zemplén, Heves and Nógrád. A survey of 1842 counted more than 2.4 million people in its counties, 59.5 per cent of them Slovaks, 22 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, 8.3 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, 6.7 per cent Germans and 3.6 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was formed at the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> it first claimed all of Upper Hungary, on the grounds of the Slovak population there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After World War I Felvidék came to mean the Slovak and Ruthenian parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The three counties left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> are called Northern Hungary, never Upper Hungary. Slovaks consider the use of Felvidék for all of Slovakia offensive, though the Hungarian minority in southern Slovakia uses it for the areas where they live, and some call themselves felvidéki magyarok, Upland Hungarians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mining brought exceptional revenues: silver from Kutná Hora and from Joachimsthal (Jáchymov), whose coins gave the thaler and eventually the dollar its name, mercury from Idrija, copper and gold from the mining towns of <b>Upper Hungary</b>, and salt from the Salzkammergut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Towns were small by western standards and often German-speaking, with Buda, Pozsony (Bratislava), Kassa (Košice) and the mining towns of <b>Upper Hungary</b> the most important.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After a string of victories he captured Kassa and much of <b>Upper Hungary</b>, and in 1682 Sultan Mehmed IV recognised him as king of Upper Hungary in return for tribute; Thököly himself used the title of prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
